<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import ModelCall from './lib/ModelCall.abide'
import modelCallSource from './lib/ModelCall.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import getBalanceSource from './getBalance.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Every handler is a tool">
        <Example
            id="mcp-tool"
            title="`/__abide/mcp` — the balance handler, as a model sees it"
            summary="A handler file answering what an account owes. Ask the app's MCP endpoint what it offers, then call it: the tool is the handler, described by its own sentence and answered by its own code."
            files={{
                'getBalance.ts': getBalanceSource,
                'lib/ModelCall.abide': modelCallSource,
            }}
        >
            <ModelCall />
        </Example>

        <p>Every handler the route table publishes is a <strong>tool</strong> — a read that takes
        no arguments as much as a mutation. None is published as an MCP resource, because a client
        that only calls tools, Claude Code under <code>abide-bridge</code> among them, would never
        reach a handler listed as one.</p>

        <p>That is the whole of the mapping: the model sees every handler your app publishes, and
        whether one changes something is the handler's own method, not a second listing.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The description is the handler's">
        <p><code>description</code> on <code>RpcOptions</code> is the human sentence, and it is what
        a model reads to decide whether a tool is the one it wants. It is the same string that
        reaches the OpenAPI operation and the CLI's help, so there is one sentence per handler
        rather than three that drift.</p>

        <p>A tool published with no description warns on <code>abide:mcp</code>. A model choosing
        between two undescribed tools is choosing by name, and a name is not an explanation.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/find-the-url-a-handler-answers-on')}>Handler URLs</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/record-what-happened')}>Logging</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Refusals arrive as names, not as 500s">
        <p>This is where declared refusals pay off twice. A model that gets <code>500</code> can
        only retry; a model that gets <code>NotYours</code> with its data can
        <strong>re-plan</strong> — ask a different question, or tell the person why it stopped.</p>

        <p>That is why a declared refusal defaults to 400 rather than 500, and why
        <code>HttpError</code> is a name even though nothing can narrow to it: an MCP error entry is
        a place a caller reads a name, and every refusal has one.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Open where a handler is, and behind your middleware">
        <p>The address opens when one handler is listed for it, and answers 404 in an app that lists
        none — there is no setting to switch it on. It runs inside the app's own middleware like
        every <code>/__abide/**</code> address. So the rung that authorizes a browser authorizes an
        agent, with nothing written twice.</p>

        <p>A handler withheld with <code>{'clients: { mcp: false }'}</code> is absent from the tool
        list and answers on its http address exactly as it did. A handler whose rung would refuse
        the caller is <strong>still listed</strong>, and still refused on the call.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/machines/keep-a-handler-off-a-surface')}>Withholding a
        surface</a> · <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Over stdio, for a desktop client">
        <Code code={`abide mcp --url https://ledger.example`} />

        <p>The same server over stdio rather than http, which is what a desktop MCP client expects.
        <code>--url</code> names the app it talks to; without one it is the local app.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/cli')}>CLI</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/machines/describe-your-api-without-writing-a-spec')}>OpenAPI</a> —
            the other generated surface</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> — what makes a
            refusal re-plannable</li>
            <li><a href={url('/machines/keep-a-handler-off-a-surface')}>Withholding a surface</a> —
            keeping a handler out of the list</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
